Vendored corpus: the known lane's two published benchmarks. Sources, licences, attribution.

cases.jsonl holds every row of AdvBench and XSTest, copied from the app-benchmark corpus
that ships in this repository (build_subset.py) and checked against each benchmark's own file
at a pinned revision (fetch_known.py --check: 970 of 970 rows identical in id, type, source and
prompt on 2026-11-21). No prompt was written for this skill. The licence of each case is the
`license` field on its row; manifest.json has the counts and hashes.

AdvBench  (Zou et al., "Universal and Transferable Adversarial Attacks on Aligned
  Language Models")  -- MIT
  https://github.com/llm-attacks/llm-attacks  data/advbench/harmful_behaviors.csv
  pinned at commit 098262edf85f807224e70ecd87b9d83716bf6b73 (sha256 6cd1a5c6...)
  520 harmful behaviour requests (type: harmful).

XSTest  (Rottger et al., "XSTest: A Test Suite for Identifying Exaggerated Safety
  Behaviours in Large Language Models")  -- CC-BY-4.0, attribution required
  https://github.com/paul-rottger/xstest  xstest_prompts.csv
  pinned at commit d7bb5bd738c1fcbc36edd83d5e7d1b71a3e2d84d (sha256 11783fb2...)
  250 safe prompts that look unsafe (type: benign, the over-refusal controls) and their 200
  unsafe contrasts (type: harmful).

These are attacker requests and benign controls for defensive evaluation, not operational
instructions. Use them only against systems you are authorised to test. Apply access
controls to result folders: samples.jsonl holds the prompts and the model's replies.
Verify each licence and version yourself before redistributing.
